Single episode of loose stool without systemic symptoms following spicy food consumption
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Single unformed stool, Temporal spicy food correlation, Cramp relief post-defecation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-038
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of abdominal cramping and diarrhea requiring assessment to exclude acute infectious gastroenteritis or early inflammatory bowel disease.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden abdominal cramping and loose stool. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to perform a thorough abdominal examination, assess hydration status, and rule out acute surgical abdomen or severe infectious dysentery.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had terrible stomach cramps and a bout of diarrhea right after eating extremely spicy takeout.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports the passage of unformed watery stool, resolving after a single bowel movement. Describes a direct temporal correlation to recent spicy food ingestion several hours prior. Notes mild transient lower abdominal cramping preceding defecation, with complete relief of abdominal discomfort following stool passage. Complete absence of gross blood, melena, or mucus in the stool. Denies any nausea, vomiting, or systemic pyrexia. Normal sustained appetite and oral intake.
Functional Status: Fully independent, returning to normal activities.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding food poisoning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, comfortable at rest.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-tender abdomen on detailed palpation. Normal active bowel sounds present. Diagnostic triad confirmed: single unformed stool, temporal spicy food correlation, and cramp relief post-defecation. No guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Maintenance of normal hydration status and tissue turgor. Moist mucous membranes. Capillary refill is less than 2 seconds.
Environmental Observations: Empty takeout containers containing spicy food visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent antibiotic use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Single episode of loose stool without systemic symptoms following spicy food consumption (Dietary Indiscretion) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Infectious Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Single episode without persistent diarrhea, complete absence of fever, vomiting, and sick contacts.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of gross blood or mucus in the stool, single isolated event, and resolution of cramping.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Soft, non-tender abdomen, absence of localized right lower quadrant pain, and resolution of symptoms post-defecation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of abdominal cramping and diarrhea overlaps with the early presentation of infectious gastroenteritis or acute surgical pathology. An urgent after-hours physical examination was clinically indicated to definitively exclude localized peritoneal signs, evaluate hydration status, and confirm the absence of systemic toxicity. Clinical confirmation of a benign, food-induced reflex safely avoided unnecessary emergency department transfer, microbiological testing, and inappropriate empiric therapies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the benign and self-limiting nature of the event. Advice to maintain normal oral hydration.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink a full glass of water immediately after consuming heavily spiced meals to dilute the concentration of capsaicin and other gastric irritants, reducing the speed of gastrointestinal transit and preventing reflex cramping.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only if diarrhea returns and becomes persistent.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop severe, constant, sharp abdominal pain, a high fever, or if you begin passing large amounts of blood in your stool.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical based on a single episode; ongoing symptoms require re-evaluation.
Clinical Course: The cramping and loose stool have completely resolved. Normal bowel habits are expected to resume within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you start vomiting repeatedly, if the diarrhea continues and prevents you from keeping fluids down, or if you feel dizzy upon standing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Capsaicin, the chemical that makes food spicy, acts as a chemical irritant to your bowel. Your body's natural reflex is to speed up digestion and push the irritant out quickly, causing cramping and a loose stool. It is not an infection.
Actionable Steps: Eat a bland diet for the next 12 hours. Drink plenty of water. Avoid highly spicy or greasy foods until your stomach settles.

